# Security Patterns

When generating security rules, incorporate these patterns adapted to language/framework and risk level.

## Secret Comparison Strategy

TimingAttackProblem {
  String comparison short-circuits on first mismatch.
  Attackers measure response time to guess character-by-character.
}

WrongSolutions {
  timingSafeEqual => still leaks length
  XOR accumulation => implementation bugs
  Direct comparison => timing oracle
}

RightSolution {
  Hash both values, then compare hashes.
  SHA3 removes prefix structure.
  Any bit change randomizes hash.
  Raw secrets never in logs/errors.
}

(secret comparison needed) => {
  hashBothValues |> compareHashes
  Add comment explaining why NOT to "fix" to timingSafeEqual
}

hashComparePattern() {
  """
  // Node.js
  const hashSecret = s => crypto.createHash('sha3-256').update(s).digest('hex');
  const verify = (stored, candidate) => hashSecret(stored) === hashSecret(candidate);

  // Python
  def hash_secret(s): return hashlib.sha3_256(s.encode()).hexdigest()
  def verify(stored, candidate): return hash_secret(stored) == hash_secret(candidate)
  """
}

## Input Validation

ValidationRules {
  Boundary => validate at system entry (forms, APIs, external data)
  Whitelist => accept known-good, not reject known-bad
}

(blacklist pattern: reject if contains X) => suggest whitelist

ValidationByType {
  Strings => length limits, character whitelist, format regex
  Numbers => range limits, type checks
  URLs => protocol whitelist, domain validation
  Files => magic bytes not extension, size limits
}

## Output Encoding

EncodingByContext {
  HTMLBody => entity encoding
  HTMLAttributes => attribute encoding + quote
  JavaScript => JS string encoding
  SQL => parameterized queries
}

(encoding bypass: dangerouslySetInnerHTML, |safe, raw) => require justification

## SQL Injection

(SQL string concatenation) => CRITICAL, require parameterized

parameterizedPattern() {
  """
  // BAD
  query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = " + userId)

  // GOOD
  query("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?", [userId])
  """
}

## Authentication Patterns

AuthRules {
  Passwords => adaptive hashing (bcrypt, argon2), never plaintext
  Sessions => cryptographically random, regenerate on privilege change
  Cookies => HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite
  RateLimiting => limit attempts, consider lockout
}

## OWASP Top 10

OWASPChecklist {
  1. BrokenAccessControl => auth on all protected resources
  2. CryptographicFailures => encryption, no secrets in code
  3. Injection => parameterized queries, input validation
  4. InsecureDesign => threat modeling
  5. SecurityMisconfiguration => change defaults
  6. VulnerableComponents => update dependencies
  7. AuthenticationFailures => strong auth, secure sessions
  8. DataIntegrityFailures => input validation, signed data
  9. LoggingFailures => log security events, no sensitive data
  10. SSRF => URL validation
}

## Risk Level Calibration

| Pattern | Personal | Internal | Business | Financial | Healthcare |
|---------|----------|----------|----------|-----------|------------|
| Secret hashing | Basic | Required | Required | Required | Required |
| Input validation | Minimal | Mistakes | Malice | Paranoid | Paranoid |
| Output encoding | Basic | Basic | Required | Required | Required |
| OWASP checks | Skip | Partial | Full | Full+audit | Compliance |

## Detection Severity

SeverityLevels {
  Critical => raw secret compare, SQL concat, secrets in code, missing auth
  Warning => encoding bypass, dynamic queries, unvalidated URLs
  Info => new auth logic, new data handling, external integrations
}

(critical pattern detected) => block/fail
(warning pattern detected) => require review
